Permissions |\n| --- | --- |\n| `0x200800000..0x200c00000` | Read/write |\n| `0x200c00000..0x201200000` | No access |\n\nThere are 12,072 bytes left from `x6` to the writable boundary. The driver is\npreparing to embed 21,384 bytes and first zeroes the final 4-byte word. That\nwrite is 9,308 bytes beyond the writable boundary. The next instructions\nwould call `memcpy` from the host source into this same internal buffer.\n\nThe driver source register is `0xedbbfb66b800`, exactly matching zero-based\ndescriptor 124 in the original K batch:\n\n```\nsource      0xedbbfb66b800\ndestination 0x433b635030\nsize        21384\n```\n\nThe fault address is neither this source nor this destination. The source lies\ninside the registered hugepage mapping `hp_tensor_kv_3`.\n\n## Checks Against the Core and Source\n\n- K batch: 142 descriptors, 40,356,064 bytes; V batch: 140 descriptors,\n  40,250,552 bytes. All three vector lengths match on each side; capacity is\n  29,134 entries. No null/negative-sized descriptor was found.\n- Every source interval is within a readable core mapping. This does not by\n  itself prove CUDA registration/lifetime, so ownership was checked separately.\n- Destination intervals do not overlap within either batch.\n- The plan represents 1,723 storage rows and binds ring 0. Its storage remains\n  owned, has readers acquired, and is not marked poisoned.\n- All 107 retained segment leases have matching generations, are allocated,\n  and have positive reader counts. No released-segment evidence was found.\n- Submission holds the native plan mutex. Sources are retained through the\n  H2D completion event; the failed plan had not finished its first submission.\n- Source uses one attribute with `srcAccessOrder=Stream`, attribute index 0,\n  and the CUDA 13 eight-argument signature. Build headers and loaded runtime\n  are CUDA 13.2; the CUDA 12/13 argument-count mismatch does not match this case.\n- The cgroup reported `oom=0`, `oom_kill=0`; no corresponding kernel OOM/Xid\n  message was found at the failure time.\n\nCUDA's API contract permits the one-attribute index arrangement and requires\nsource lifetime through stream consumption:\nhttps://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/13.2.1/cuda-driver-api/group__CUDA__MEM.html\n\n## Diagnostic Runs and Limits\n\nA standalone C++ CUDA test used the original K descriptor sizes and, after\nthe initial aligned test, the original source/destination offsets modulo\n64 KiB. Each allocation remained live through stream synchronization. All\n10,000-iteration cases below passed with a final copied-data check:\n\n1. CUDA-pinned host allocation, aligned addresses.\n2. CUDA-pinned host allocation, original address alignment.\n3. Registered anonymous host mapping.\n4. Registered mapping with Nsight 2026.2.1 injection, profiler start/stop,\n   and the original precapture mode; copies continue after capture stops.\n5. Registered 16 GiB hugepage, bound to NUMA node 0.\n6. Registered hugepage with GPU virtual memory backed by 2 MiB allocations,\n   approximating the expandable-segment allocator used by the workload.\n\nThese are focused diagnostics, not reproductions of the full serving state.\nThey do not establish that the original bug is fixed or that a driver update,\ndisabling nsys, or replacing batched copies would fix it. They omit the original\nsequence of changing batches, model kernels, concurrent encoders, and long-lived\nallocator/profiler state. Final resource check: GPU 36 MiB / 0%, 23 hugepages free.\n\n## Remaining Discriminating Work\n\nBefore another full serving run, preserve the original core because the next\ncrash can overwrite `core`. A controlled rerun of the failing cell needs native\nbatch-call records immediately before submission (copy arguments and source\nownership), plus an A/B without Nsight and, separately, without the custom\n`stack_pool.so` memory interposer. The interposer's global free-list is not\nthread-safe and ignores mapping ownership/protection when poolin